Here is an optimized .jsx script for InDesign that transforms a spread document with multiple master pages into a single page document:

```jsx
app.scriptPreferences.userInteractionLevel = UserInteractionLevels.INTERACT_WITH_ALL;

var doc = app.activeDocument;
var spreads = doc.spreads;

for (var i =  spreads.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    var spread = spreads[i];
    for (var j = spread.pages.length - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
        var page = spread.pages[j];
        var newPage = doc.pages.add(LocationOptions.AT_BEGINNING, doc.pages[0]);
        newPage.appliedMaster = page.appliedMaster;
        newPage.marginPreferences = page.marginPreferences;
        for (var k = page.pageItems.length - 1; k >= 0; k--) {
            var item = page.pageItems[k];
            item.duplicate(newPage);
        }
    }
    spread.remove();
}
```

This script loops through all spreads in the document, duplicates each page onto a new single page, and applies the same master page, margin preferences, and page items. It then removes the original spreads from the document.

Please note that this script assumes that all pages in the spreads have the same master page applied. If your document contains pages with different master pages applied, you may need to add additional logic to handle this scenario.

